\ `drop-at` indexes the stack at a depth supplied at runtime, so its body's
\ `<rot-n` has no literal depth to fold and compiles as a native call against the
\ live stack that collapses the abstract stack to a row region. Under runtime-image
\ AOT the word body is compiled, so these results come from the compiled path and
\ match the interpreter: a mid-stack drop, a depth-0 drop of the top, a depth-1
\ drop one below the top, and an out-of-range depth raising the same
\ `stack-underflow` the interpreter does, caught with `recover`.
1 2 3 4 2 drop-at . . .
"---" print-line
1 2 3 0 drop-at . .
"---" print-line
1 2 3 4 5 1 drop-at . . . .
"---" print-line
[ 1 2 10 drop-at ] [ "error-type" @get inspect print-line ] recover
